  "body": "## What changed\n\nA milling setup's WCS can be turned onto another face from the WCS pane, but every milling generator cuts along the machine's Z. The turned WCS was drawn and then ignored: the reporter's Setup2 facing machined the face that is level in the model (the wrong face) and the op said \"OK\". The post already refused any rotated work offset (#315), so no wrong G-code could post. The toolpath was still wrong, though, and nothing on screen said so.\n\n- New `crates/app/src/setup_wcs_tilt.rs`: `generation_problem` / `setup_problem` return a named refusal when a milling setup's WCS Z is tilted off the machine's +Z (more than 1e-9 rad, the post's identity tolerance).\n- `collect_job` (the generation door) refuses with it: *\"setup 'Setup2': its WCS (G54) has its Z axis tilted 90.000 deg off the machine's Z - the tool always cuts along the machine's Z, so this would machine the part as it lies, not as the WCS shows; turn the part with the setup's Part alignment instead and keep the WCS Z pointing up\"*.\n- A facing pick on a face that is level only in the tilted WCS (the reporter's first error) now names the WCS as the reason instead of suggesting a 2D Contour. It keeps the \"is not horizontal - a facing pass needs a floor\" door phrase, so `door_refusal` still treats it as a door refusal.\n- A WCS turned only about Z (a squared-up offset) still generates. Its post refusal is #315's, unchanged.\n- Milling only (`Kind::Mill`). Lathe, wire and the other machine kinds are not affected.\n- Decision recorded in `docs/decisions/1510-tilted-wcs.md`. Actually generating in the WCS frame was considered and dropped: that is effort:max work, and Part alignment (#326) already turns the part for a setup.\n\n## How tested\n\nPinning tests:\n- `crates/app/tests/wcs_tilt.rs::a_tilted_setup_wcs_refuses_generation_by_name`: the reporter's case (Setup1 square, Setup2 WCS turned 90 deg about X). Setup2's face is refused by name and Setup1's still generates.\n- `crates/app/tests/wcs_tilt.rs::a_wcs_turned_only_about_z_still_generates`: the scope guard (yaw only still generates).\n- `crates/app/tests/pick_doors.rs::a_wall_level_only_in_a_tilted_wcs_names_the_wcs`: the pick refusal names the tilted WCS and Part alignment.\n\nMutation check (standing rule):\n- Deleted the `setup_wcs_tilt::generation_problem` check in `collect_job`: `a_tilted_setup_wcs_refuses_generation_by_name` went RED. Restored: GREEN.\n- Deleted the tilted-WCS branch in `selection.rs`: `a_wall_level_only_in_a_tilted_wcs_names_the_wcs` went RED. Restored: GREEN.\n\nAlso run by hand, all green: rotated_wcs, commands_undo, mcp_model, mcp_setup, mcp_wcs_features, one_wcs, project_roundtrip, rotary_verify, wcs_align, wcs_roles, posting, setup_sheet, setups, heights, setup_wcs, setup_defines_wcs, wcs_view_pose, circle_centre, face_origin, operations, setup_align.\n\nQuick gate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`. green: 1 step (19 app suites + app lib), 0 failed, 4.3 min wall; `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The GUI by hand. The refusal reaches the op status and status line the way every `GenError::Invalid` does, and no UI code changed, so there are no structural assertions or snapshots to update.\n- Ops in a non-mill setup under a tilted WCS. They are out of scope on purpose.\n\nChangelog: A milling setup whose WCS Z is tilted off the machine's Z is now refused at generation by name (pointing at Part alignment), instead of quietly facing the wrong face; a facing pick that is level only in the tilted WCS says why (#1510)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nfork synced at v0.19.0 (branch merged with upstream/main for the PR; conflicts were only the fork's process-sync files, upstream's taken)\n\nFixes #1510\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
